{"title":"Christmas in Old York! 1833 (RETURN FOR 2026)","startsAt":"2026-11-28T12:00:00-05:00","endsAt":"2026-11-28T14:00:00-05:00","status":"ACTIVE","type":"PHYSICAL","description":"\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Yearly Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours, email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or for signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\n**If you plan on attending, please get your tickets for this event early as this tour has run for the last 3 years, and does sell out rather quickly.**\n\nSo what was it like in York in 1833 at Christmas time?\n\nThe prices of various food items and gifts on display, the gas lights creating a glow as the snow begins, places to gather, places to get a drink, like the Steamboat Hotel on Front Street, or maybe one might decide to take their best girl or guy on a stagecoach trip to High Park to watch kids speed down the hills on their skids.\n\nWhat were the traditions in old York? Maybe the residents went down to the lake and watched the nighttime fishermen spear fish for Christmas dinner. Let's take a trip around old York and see what it was like back in 1833, three months before we changed our name to Toronto in 1834.\n\nOr take a holiday trip to Kingston or Montreal to buy gifts directly from New York or London stores, fancy hats for the ladies and cigars for the men. Books and wooden toys for the kids. What about that goose in the little grocery store on King Street West of Jarvis? Candies from Britain, real home made gingerbread in the store windows.\n\nOn Christmas Eve everyone walked to St. James Cathedral for the ringing of the bells, singing, and skating over the frozen Toronto Bay to the Peninsula.\n\nThis tour explores old York. This walking tour is for all walking levels. The tour begins and ends at St. James Cathedral on King Street East at Church Street. A great way to kick off the season.\n\nThis tour has a ticketed price of $15.00.\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**","venue":{"name":"The Cathedral Church of St. James","address":"106 King St E, Toronto","lat":43.650383,"lng":-79.37396,"city":"Toronto"},"fee":{"amount":15,"currency":"CAD"},"group":"Toronto History Walks","image":"https://secure.meetupstatic.com/photos/event/3/7/e/3/highres_499514307.jpeg"}